# A golden archive produced by a real tar: git must not touch a byte of it,
# and core.autocrlf here would.
*.tar binary

# A shell script with CRLF does not run: the interpreter reads the carriage return as part of
# the first line and answers "end of file unexpected". core.autocrlf is true on the machine this
# was written on, so a checkout reintroduced it into a file that had been committed LF, and the
# wrapper is the half of this repository a consumer copies into their own project.
*.sh text eol=lf
wrapper/daukle text eol=lf
wrapper/.daukle/wrapper.toml text eol=lf
# The PowerShell half too, and not because PowerShell minds: these three files are COPIED
# into other repositories, and daukle/examples pins `* -text` so its copy keeps whatever
# bytes were committed. Left unpinned here, autocrlf gave the two repositories different
# bytes for one file and the examples harness reported a drift that was only line endings.
wrapper/daukle.ps1 text eol=lf
